# Counted Proof Spaces: An Interactive Journey

Begin with the central tension: a structured family can grow rapidly and still disappear in relative density because its ambient language grows faster. Explore the paired observables directly.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

## Build the counting model

The ambient volume is $S_k(n)=1+k+\cdots+k^n$. The following algorithm computes it exactly and supplies the denominator for every later experiment.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

Continue with an exact rational crossing calculation and a direct ratio check.

{{demo:1}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the entropy–sparsity argument</summary>
If $P(n)\le Ca^n$ and $a<k$, then $S_k(n)\ge k^n$ gives $0\le\rho(n)\le C(a/k)^n\to0$. Meanwhile $k^n\le S_k(n)\le k^{n+1}$ implies $\log S_k(n)/n\to\log k$. Thus the phase vector tends to $(0,\log k)$.
</details>

## Find a sharp crossing

Convergence alone allows oscillation. Antitone density turns eventual decay into a unique boundary. Move the level and watch the critical index change.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the uniqueness proof</summary>
Because density tends to zero, it is eventually below every positive $\varepsilon$. Since it starts at or above the admissible level and never increases, there is a last index $c$ with $\rho(c)\ge\varepsilon$. Then $\rho(n)<\varepsilon$ holds exactly when $n>c$, which also proves uniqueness.
</details>

## Test the power-law claim

A homogeneous entropy model has geometric masses and constant ratio $1/k$. Compare that signature with the changing ratio of a power law.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal why mixtures matter</summary>
A single exponential rate remains geometric. A continuum of rates concentrated near zero can instead produce a heavy aggregate tail: the Laplace integral $\int_0^\infty e^{-\lambda n}\lambda^{\alpha-1}d\lambda=\Gamma(\alpha)n^{-\alpha}$ illustrates the mechanism. This motivates heterogeneous proof regimes rather than deriving a power law from syntax alone.
</details>

## Further reading

For background, see [geometric series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geometric_series), [information entropy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entropy_(information_theory)), [phase transitions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phase_transition), and [regular variation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regularly_varying_function). These links are optional context; all results used above are stated in this presentation.
